\begin{abstract}
The MK-15 suprakernel simulation architecture unifies flight, structural,
thermal, propulsion, and control dynamics into a single encrypted,
ledger-bound, perturbation-damped runtime. This paper presents the
mathematical formulation of the suprakernel state, the constraint
envelope governing MK-15 evolution, the thermal shock gradient operator,
and the perturbation damping mechanism. Results demonstrate stable
hypersonic evolution in the Mach 10--15 regime under strict thermal and
structural constraints. The suprakernel framework provides a foundation
for high-stress trans-atmospheric simulation and future extended-domain
coupling.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
High-speed trans-atmospheric vehicles require simulation architectures
capable of integrating multiple physical domains under extreme
conditions. Traditional aerospace simulation frameworks treat flight
mechanics, structural dynamics, thermal loads, propulsion, and control
systems as separate modules. This fragmentation introduces coupling
errors and limits stability at hypersonic speeds.

The MK-15 suprakernel architecture addresses these limitations by
providing a unified multi-domain simulation organism. Each iteration is
governed by a constraint envelope and verified through encrypted
ledger-bound state evolution. This paper formalizes the MK-15
suprakernel and evaluates its stability in the Mach 10--15 regime.

\section{Background}
Conventional simulation approaches rely on modular solvers that exchange
boundary conditions between subsystems. While effective for moderate
flight regimes, these methods struggle with the nonlinear coupling
present in hypersonic and trans-atmospheric environments. Recent
research has explored integrated solvers, but none incorporate
cryptographic verification or perturbation-damped evolution.

The suprakernel architecture extends integrated simulation by enforcing
global constraints, damping perturbations, and binding each state to an
encrypted ledger. This ensures stability, reproducibility, and
traceability across all domains.

\section{Methods}

\subsection{Global MK-15 State}
The MK-15 suprakernel state is defined as
\[
\mathbf{X}_{\text{MK15}} =
\begin{bmatrix}
\mathbf{X}_{\text{flight}} \\
\mathbf{X}_{\text{struct}} \\
\mathbf{X}_{\text{thermal}} \\
\mathbf{X}_{\text{prop}} \\
\mathbf{X}_{\text{ctrl}}
\end{bmatrix}.
\]

\subsection{State Evolution}
The suprakernel advances the MK-15 state using
\[
\mathbf{X}_{\text{MK15}}^{(n+1)} =
\mathcal{F}_{\text{MK15}}\big(\mathbf{X}_{\text{MK15}}^{(n)}, \Delta t\big),
\]
where $\mathcal{F}_{\text{MK15}}$ integrates aerodynamic loads,
structural response, thermal flux, propulsion dynamics, and control
inputs.

\subsection{Thermal Shock Gradient}
Thermal shock susceptibility is evaluated using
\[
G_{\text{TS}} =
\delta_{1}\lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{K}_{\text{th}})
-
\delta_{2}\lambda_{\max}(\mathbf{C}_{\text{th}})
-
\delta_{3}\lambda_{\max}(\mathbf{S}_{\text{shock}}),
\]
with shock margin
\[
\text{SM}_{\text{TS}} = G_{\text{TS}} - G_{\text{crit,TS}}.
\]

\subsection{Perturbation Damping}
Perturbations evolve under
\[
\delta \mathbf{X}' =
\mathcal{D}_{\text{pert}}\big(\delta \mathbf{X}\big),
\quad
\|\delta \mathbf{X}'\| \le \kappa \|\delta \mathbf{X}\|,
\]
with $0 < \kappa < 1$ enforcing suprakernel stability.

\subsection{Encrypted Runtime}
Encrypted MK-15 state:
\[
\Lambda^{(n)} =
\mathcal{E}_{\text{MK15}}\big(\mathbf{X}_{\text{MK15}}^{(n)}, k_{\text{sec}}\big).
\]

Ledger entry:
\[
\ell^{(n)} =
\mathcal{L}_{\text{MK15}}\big(\mathbf{X}_{\text{MK15}}^{(n)}, \Lambda^{(n)}\big).
\]

Hash chain:
\[
H^{(n)} =
\text{Hash}\big(\ell^{(n)} \,\|\, H^{(n-1)}\big).
\]

\subsection{Constraint Envelope}
All updates must satisfy
\[
\mathbf{X}_{\text{MK15}}^{(n)} \in
\mathcal{C}_{\text{flight}}
\wedge
\mathcal{C}_{\text{thermal}}
\wedge
\mathcal{C}_{\text{struct}}
\wedge
\mathcal{C}_{\text{prop}}
\wedge
\mathcal{C}_{\text{ctrl}}
\wedge
\mathcal{C}_{\text{enc}}
\wedge
\mathcal{C}_{\text{ledger}}.
\]

\section{Results}
Simulation of the MK-15 suprakernel in the Mach 10--15 regime produced
stable evolution across all domains. Thermal shock margins remained
positive under high flux conditions, and perturbation damping prevented
divergence during aerodynamic transients.

Encrypted ledger verification ensured reproducibility across all
iterations. Structural loads remained within allowable limits, and
propulsion dynamics maintained torque stability.

\section{Discussion}
The MK-15 suprakernel demonstrates that unified multi-domain simulation
can maintain stability in extreme flight regimes. The thermal shock
gradient operator provides a robust measure of thermal susceptibility,
while perturbation damping ensures resilience against transient
instabilities.

The encrypted runtime and ledger system introduce a novel mechanism for
traceability and verification, enabling secure simulation pipelines for
high-stress aerospace applications.

\section{Conclusion}
The MK-15 suprakernel architecture provides a unified, encrypted,
perturbation-damped simulation framework capable of supporting
trans-atmospheric hypersonic vehicles. Future work will extend the
architecture to inter-domain coupling and FTL kernel integration.

